<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minor C. Keith</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Minor Cooper Keith</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minor Cooper Keith was an American businessman who built the railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>'s Central Valley to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast between 1871 and 1890. He planted bananas along the line to provide freight, and in 1899 his banana business merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>. He went on to control railways and plantations across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minor-c-keith.jpg" alt="Minor C. Keith" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minor_Cooper_Keith_in_1917.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Minor Cooper Keith was born on 19 January 1848 in Brooklyn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, the son of the lumber merchant Minor Hubbell Keith and his wife Emily, a sister of the railroad entrepreneur <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-meiggs">Henry Meiggs</a>. At sixteen he worked as a store clerk on Broadway and then as a lumber surveyor. With $3,000 saved in a year he bought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> ranch on a river island near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, which he ran until 1871, when his uncle invited him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Meiggs had contracted with the government of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-guardia-gutierrez">Tomás Guardia Gutiérrez</a> to build a railroad from San José to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast at Limón, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>'s coffee a route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Keith took the project over after Meiggs died in 1877. Poor financing, jungle, rain and disease made the work very hard: as many as four thousand people, among them Keith's three brothers, died building the first 25 miles of track, mostly of malaria. Keith brought in labourers from abroad, including Jamaicans, Chinese and Italians.</p><p>When the government defaulted in 1882, Keith raised £1.2 million himself and negotiated lower interest on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>'s London loans. In return the Soto-Keith contract of 21 April 1884 gave him 800,000 acres of tax-free land along the line, about 6% of the country, and a 99-year lease on the railroad. In 1883 he married Cristina Castro Fernández, daughter of the former president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-castro-madriz">José María Castro Madriz</a> and niece of President Próspero Fernández Oreamuno.</p><p>Keith had begun planting bananas as early as 1873 and shipped them by steamboat from Limón to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a> through his Tropical Trading and Transport Company, later adding plantations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>'s Magdalena Department. A financial setback forced the 1899 merger with Andrew W. Preston's Boston Fruit Company that created United Fruit, of which he became vice-president. In 1904 he won from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-estrada-cabrera">Manuel Estrada Cabrera</a> tax exemptions, land and control of the Atlantic railroads, and from 1912 he built 800 miles of track for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-railways-of-central-america">International Railways of Central America</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Keith also mined gold in Abangares and developed land around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-city">Panama City</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. He bequeathed his collection of ancient Native American gold to the American Museum of Natural History, and died of bronchial pneumonia at West Islip, Long Island, on 14 June 1929, before realising his plan for a line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minor Cooper Keith” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tomás Guardia's rule from 1870 began the railway to Limón, built by <b>Minor Keith</b>, which spawned the banana industry.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-costa-rica">Republic of Costa Rica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brooklyn, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in West Islip, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.65,-73.95&amp;year=1900" aria-label="Brooklyn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Minor C. Keith"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4104 4040h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4104 4040h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.65,-73.95&amp;year=1900">What was here in 1900? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
